window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-12-3", "source": "2022年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: In this task, you are to write an essay on the necessity of developing social skills for college students. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "In today's interconnected campus life, the necessity of developing social skills for college students can hardly\nbe overstated. Far from being an optional extra, the ability to communicate and cooperate is indispensable to a\nstudent's growth.\nTo begin with, strong social skills lie at the heart of academic success. Much college learning happens through\ngroup projects, seminars and lab work, where students who express ideas clearly and listen to others achieve\nfar more than those who work in isolation. Moreover, these skills pave the way for future careers. Employers\ntoday value teamwork and communication as highly as professional knowledge, and graduates who can\nnetwork and collaborate stand out in interviews. Equally important, good social skills help students build\nfriendships, ease loneliness and adapt to campus life smoothly.\nTherefore, developing social skills is by no means a trivial matter but a genuine necessity for every college\nstudent. Universities and students alike should treat it as seriously as academic study, so that young people can\nthrive both on campus and beyond."}, "translation": {"prompt": "冬至(Winter Solstice)是全年白昼最短、黑夜最长的一天，标志着一年中最寒冷时节的开始。冬至过后，气温越来越低，人们的户外活动逐渐减少。农民地里活儿不多，主要忙于灌溉系统的维护和农作物的防冻，同时为来年春天播种做准备。 中国人历来很重视冬至，许多地方都把冬至当作一个节日，庆祝方式各地不尽相同。北方人有冬至吃饺子(jiaozi)的习俗，南方人有冬至吃汤圆(tangyuan)的传统。", "sample": "The Winter Solstice is the day on which daytime is the shortest and night is the longest in the whole year, marking the beginning of the coldest period of the year. After the Winter Solstice, the temperature drops lower and lower, and people's outdoor activities gradually decrease. Since there is not much work to do in the fields, farmers are mainly busy with maintaining the irrigation system and protecting crops from freezing, while preparing for next spring's sowing. Chinese people have always attached great importance to the Winter Solstice and regard it as a festival in many places, though the ways of celebrating it vary from place to place. Northerners have the custom of eating jiaozi on the Winter Solstice, while Southerners have the tradition of eating tangyuan on this day."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Phones influence all aspects of teenage life. Ninety-five percent of Americans ages 13 to 17 have a smartphone or have access to one, and nearly half report using the internet \"almost  __26__  .\"\nBut as recent survey data and interviews have suggested, many teens find much of that time to be unsatisfyingly spent. Continuous  __27__  shouldn't be mistaken for endless enjoyment. A new  __28__  representative survey about \"screen time and device distractions\" from the Pew Research Center indicates that it's not just parents who think teenagers are worryingly  __29__  from their phones—many teens themselves do too. Fifty-four percent of the 13- to 17-year-olds surveyed said they spend too much time  __30__  in their phones.\nVicky Rideout, who runs a research firm that studies children's interactions with media and technology, was not surprised by this finding. She says it's hardly  __31__  to teenagers. \"They are dealing with the same challenges that adults are, as far as they are living in the  __32__  of a tech environment designed to suck as much of their time onto their devices as possible,\" Rideout says.\nThe way parents interact with technology can  __33__  the way they interact with their kids. Rideout thus thinks it's up to parents to model good  __34__  : Kids tend to take note if their parents put their phone away at dinner or charge it in another room while they sleep. Witnessing habits like that can help kids \"realize that they can  __35__  some more control over their devices,\" she says.", "matchIntro": "Evil Genius", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "A few years ago I found myself teaching a university class on evil. It was for third-year criminology students to help them contextualize theory and research within controversial current topics. It was a huge success. The debates were heated and interesting. I could see people's views change within the course of a single lecture. Over the past 13 years, as a student, lecturer and researcher, I've enjoyed discussing the science of evil with anyone willing to listen. What I like most is destroying the cliche(陈词滥调) of good and evil, and replacing them with scientific insight. We need a more informed way of discussing behavior that at first we cannot, or should not, begin to understand."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Without understanding, we risk dehumanizing others, writing off human beings simply because we don't comprehend them. We must try to understand what we have labeled evil. We tend to think evil is something that other people are. We think of ourselves as \"good people\", and even when we do morally wrong things, we understand the context of our decisions. With others, however, it is far easier to write them off. If their actions deviate(偏离) substantially from what we consider acceptable, we may label them evil. We need to be careful with this. Calling someone evil is often similar to saying they cannot change, and perhaps aren't even a human at all. However, when you actually go monster-hunting, and you look deeply at the people behind shocking behavior, you may be surprised."}, {"label": "C", "text": "As a child I used to love the Scooby-Doo cartoons. Arriving in their \"Mystery Machine\", the gang would have to find a monster who was terrorizing a neighborhood. They would run around looking for clues and at the end unmask the bad guy. It was always a normal person in a costume. There were no monsters. Like the Scooby crew, we may find ourselves hunting for an easy fix, one word for people who do bad things. But if we take a good look, the word \"evil\" is insufficient—there are no simple explanations for why humans do bad things: instead there are many, and they are all marvelously different."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Evil is typically referred to when there is deviance from social norms: formal deviance is the violation of laws, like theft, murder, and attacks, while informal deviance involves violations of social norms, like lying. Evil behavior is typically thought to embrace one or both forms. However, deviance can also describe a behavior that simply differs from the norm."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Perhaps this is where we can find the good side of our bad side. Deviating from the norm can make us villains(恶棍), but it can also make us heroes. A child deviates from social pressures when they stand up for another child being bullied in school. A soldier deviates when they choose not to follow orders to kill an innocent civilian. An employee in a big tech company deviates when they expose its wrongdoings."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Creativity is also a deviation. Here, too, things are complex. Thinking creatively has given us modern medicine, technology and modern political structures, but it has also given us poison and nuclear weapons. Great benefit and great harm can come from the same human tendency."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In a research paper, Evil Genius, published in 2014, the behavioral scientists Francesca Gino and Scott Wiltermuth wanted to examine whether people who behave unethically in one task are more creative than others on a subsequent task, even after controlling for differences in baseline creative skills. The unethical behavior they chose was dishonesty."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Over five experiments, researchers gave participants tasks in which they could cheat. In one study, they were given matrices(矩阵) and had to find two numbers that added up to 10. Participants were asked to self-report how well they did at the end of the study: 59% cheated by saying that they solved more matrices than they actually had."}, {"label": "I", "text": "After each task, the researchers measured participants' performance on the Remote Associates Test. This shows participants three words at a time that appear to be unrelated, and the person has to think of a fourth word that is associated with all of them. For example, you might get \"Fox, Man, Peep\", or \"Dust, Cereal, Fish\". In order to find the linking words (\"Hole\" for the first, \"Bowl\" for the second) you need to be creative. The more you get right, the more creative you are thought to be because you have come up with uncommon associations."}, {"label": "J", "text": "For every one of the five studies, they found the same thing—participants who cheated in the first task did better on the creativity task. Why? Like other forms of unethical behavior, lying means breaking rules. It involves being deviant, going against the social principle that people should tell the truth. Similarly, being creative involves \"thinking outside the box\", deviating from expectations. They involve similar thought patterns, so stimulating one stimulates the other. Can we learn from this? Perhaps. To be more creative, we could try lying in a controlled environment. Find online logic games and cheat at them, play Scrabble(拼字游戏) with a dictionary, or write a story about something that is untrue? Such tasks can get our brains thinking flexibly, beyond our normal comfort zone. This is not a call to become a compulsive(强迫性的) liar, but a controlled liar."}, {"label": "K", "text": "In addition to benefits for creativity, deviance can be a good thing in other ways. Even Philip Zimbardo, the author of the Stanford prison experiment, who showed how easily we can be led to behave badly, believes that the future of deviance research may lie more in understanding extreme pro-social behavior, such as heroism. Like evil, we often view heroism as only a possibility for outliers—for people who are abnormal. But Zimbardo asks: \"What if the capability to act heroically is also fundamentally ordinary and available to all of us?\" Some say we should never meet our heroes, lest they disappoint us when we find out how normal they are. But this should be liberating, not disappointing. We are all capable of behaving like outliers. It's time for us to understand deviance, and realize its potential for good as well as for harm."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Even though we are living in an age where growing old is thought of as an inevitable misery, this doctor has been changing the game for seniors over the last 25 years.\nIt all started in 1991 when the Harvard-educated physician was transferred from working in a stressful emergency room to being the medical director of a nursing home in upstate New York. The depressing and regimented(严格管制的) environment got him thinking on what exactly could improve the residents' conditions.\nEven though animals in nursing homes were illegal at the time, Dr. Bill Thomas took a chance. Based on a hunch(直觉), he brought in two dogs, four cats, hens, rabbits, 100 birds, a multitude of plants, a flower garden, and a vegetable patch.\nThe change was dramatic. There was a 50% drop in medical prescriptions along with a dramatic decrease in death rates—but most importantly, the residents were simply happier.\nDr. Thomas's approach, named the Eden Alternative, has driven nursing homes to allow a more autonomous(自主的) and creative living space for their elderly. It erases the belief that growing old means growing useless. He encourages residents to think of their age as an enriching new phase of life rather than the end of it.\nThomas, now a speaker and author of several books, also created small, independently-run residences with their own bedrooms and bathrooms, and he has been preaching a singular message that getting old is not a bad thing.\n\"Within six weeks, they had to send a truck around to pick up all the wheelchairs,\" Thomas told the Washington Post. \"You know why most people in nursing homes use wheelchairs? Because the buildings are so big.\"\nThe 56-year-old doctor's methods have been adopted in Australia, Japan, Canada, and America with enormous success. Last year he published Second Wind: Navigating the Passage to a Slower, Deeper, and More Connected Life, a guide on how to shift our perspectives on aging and growth.\nHe is currently traveling through North America performing with his guitar and his enthusiasm on his Age of Disruption Tour.", "47": "Even though we are living in an age where growing old is thought of as an inevitable misery, this doctor has been changing the game for seniors over the last 25 years.\nIt all started in 1991 when the Harvard-educated physician was transferred from working in a stressful emergency room to being the medical director of a nursing home in upstate New York. The depressing and regimented(严格管制的) environment got him thinking on what exactly could improve the residents' conditions.\nEven though animals in nursing homes were illegal at the time, Dr. Bill Thomas took a chance. Based on a hunch(直觉), he brought in two dogs, four cats, hens, rabbits, 100 birds, a multitude of plants, a flower garden, and a vegetable patch.\nThe change was dramatic. There was a 50% drop in medical prescriptions along with a dramatic decrease in death rates—but most importantly, the residents were simply happier.\nDr. Thomas's approach, named the Eden Alternative, has driven nursing homes to allow a more autonomous(自主的) and creative living space for their elderly. It erases the belief that growing old means growing useless. He encourages residents to think of their age as an enriching new phase of life rather than the end of it.\nThomas, now a speaker and author of several books, also created small, independently-run residences with their own bedrooms and bathrooms, and he has been preaching a singular message that getting old is not a bad thing.\n\"Within six weeks, they had to send a truck around to pick up all the wheelchairs,\" Thomas told the Washington Post. \"You know why most people in nursing homes use wheelchairs? Because the buildings are so big.\"\nThe 56-year-old doctor's methods have been adopted in Australia, Japan, Canada, and America with enormous success. Last year he published Second Wind: Navigating the Passage to a Slower, Deeper, and More Connected Life, a guide on how to shift our perspectives on aging and growth.\nHe is currently traveling through North America performing with his guitar and his enthusiasm on his Age of Disruption Tour.", "48": "Even though we are living in an age where growing old is thought of as an inevitable misery, this doctor has been changing the game for seniors over the last 25 years.\nIt all started in 1991 when the Harvard-educated physician was transferred from working in a stressful emergency room to being the medical director of a nursing home in upstate New York. The depressing and regimented(严格管制的) environment got him thinking on what exactly could improve the residents' conditions.\nEven though animals in nursing homes were illegal at the time, Dr. Bill Thomas took a chance. Based on a hunch(直觉), he brought in two dogs, four cats, hens, rabbits, 100 birds, a multitude of plants, a flower garden, and a vegetable patch.\nThe change was dramatic. There was a 50% drop in medical prescriptions along with a dramatic decrease in death rates—but most importantly, the residents were simply happier.\nDr. Thomas's approach, named the Eden Alternative, has driven nursing homes to allow a more autonomous(自主的) and creative living space for their elderly. It erases the belief that growing old means growing useless. He encourages residents to think of their age as an enriching new phase of life rather than the end of it.\nThomas, now a speaker and author of several books, also created small, independently-run residences with their own bedrooms and bathrooms, and he has been preaching a singular message that getting old is not a bad thing.\n\"Within six weeks, they had to send a truck around to pick up all the wheelchairs,\" Thomas told the Washington Post. \"You know why most people in nursing homes use wheelchairs? Because the buildings are so big.\"\nThe 56-year-old doctor's methods have been adopted in Australia, Japan, Canada, and America with enormous success. Last year he published Second Wind: Navigating the Passage to a Slower, Deeper, and More Connected Life, a guide on how to shift our perspectives on aging and growth.\nHe is currently traveling through North America performing with his guitar and his enthusiasm on his Age of Disruption Tour.", "49": "Even though we are living in an age where growing old is thought of as an inevitable misery, this doctor has been changing the game for seniors over the last 25 years.\nIt all started in 1991 when the Harvard-educated physician was transferred from working in a stressful emergency room to being the medical director of a nursing home in upstate New York. The depressing and regimented(严格管制的) environment got him thinking on what exactly could improve the residents' conditions.\nEven though animals in nursing homes were illegal at the time, Dr. Bill Thomas took a chance. Based on a hunch(直觉), he brought in two dogs, four cats, hens, rabbits, 100 birds, a multitude of plants, a flower garden, and a vegetable patch.\nThe change was dramatic. There was a 50% drop in medical prescriptions along with a dramatic decrease in death rates—but most importantly, the residents were simply happier.\nDr. Thomas's approach, named the Eden Alternative, has driven nursing homes to allow a more autonomous(自主的) and creative living space for their elderly. It erases the belief that growing old means growing useless. He encourages residents to think of their age as an enriching new phase of life rather than the end of it.\nThomas, now a speaker and author of several books, also created small, independently-run residences with their own bedrooms and bathrooms, and he has been preaching a singular message that getting old is not a bad thing.\n\"Within six weeks, they had to send a truck around to pick up all the wheelchairs,\" Thomas told the Washington Post. \"You know why most people in nursing homes use wheelchairs? Because the buildings are so big.\"\nThe 56-year-old doctor's methods have been adopted in Australia, Japan, Canada, and America with enormous success. Last year he published Second Wind: Navigating the Passage to a Slower, Deeper, and More Connected Life, a guide on how to shift our perspectives on aging and growth.\nHe is currently traveling through North America performing with his guitar and his enthusiasm on his Age of Disruption Tour.", "50": "Even though we are living in an age where growing old is thought of as an inevitable misery, this doctor has been changing the game for seniors over the last 25 years.\nIt all started in 1991 when the Harvard-educated physician was transferred from working in a stressful emergency room to being the medical director of a nursing home in upstate New York. The depressing and regimented(严格管制的) environment got him thinking on what exactly could improve the residents' conditions.\nEven though animals in nursing homes were illegal at the time, Dr. Bill Thomas took a chance. Based on a hunch(直觉), he brought in two dogs, four cats, hens, rabbits, 100 birds, a multitude of plants, a flower garden, and a vegetable patch.\nThe change was dramatic. There was a 50% drop in medical prescriptions along with a dramatic decrease in death rates—but most importantly, the residents were simply happier.\nDr. Thomas's approach, named the Eden Alternative, has driven nursing homes to allow a more autonomous(自主的) and creative living space for their elderly. It erases the belief that growing old means growing useless. He encourages residents to think of their age as an enriching new phase of life rather than the end of it.\nThomas, now a speaker and author of several books, also created small, independently-run residences with their own bedrooms and bathrooms, and he has been preaching a singular message that getting old is not a bad thing.\n\"Within six weeks, they had to send a truck around to pick up all the wheelchairs,\" Thomas told the Washington Post. \"You know why most people in nursing homes use wheelchairs? Because the buildings are so big.\"\nThe 56-year-old doctor's methods have been adopted in Australia, Japan, Canada, and America with enormous success. Last year he published Second Wind: Navigating the Passage to a Slower, Deeper, and More Connected Life, a guide on how to shift our perspectives on aging and growth.\nHe is currently traveling through North America performing with his guitar and his enthusiasm on his Age of Disruption Tour.", "51": "Research shows that in developed countries, more affluent and educated people tend to consume higher-quality diets—including more fruits and vegetables, fish and whole grains. On the contrary, economically disadvantaged people report diets that are nutrient-poor and energy-dense. They are less likely to have food-purchasing habits that conform to public health recommendations.\nThese dietary differences are often accompanied by higher rates of obesity and diabetes among lower-income people. This relationship between social class and diet quality and health is extensively documented. However, the research does not explain why this is the case—a question that has significant implications for designing effective policies and initiatives to improve diets and prevent chronic diseases.\nPublic-health initiatives to promote healthy diets often focus on providing nutrition education and recipes(食谱). These approaches, however, often presume less food literacy (i.e., food knowledge and skills) among low-income people. Are unhealthy diets really the result of poor choices, limited food skills and knowledge?\nResearch suggests that adults in food-insecure households are just as likely as those in food-secure households to adjust recipes to make them more healthy. They are also just as proficient in food preparation and cooking skills. There is no indication that increasing food skills or budgeting skills will reduce food insecurity.\nInstead, disadvantaged groups are constrained by their economic, material and social circumstances. For example, low income is the strongest predictor of food insecurity in Canada, where one in eight households experiences insufficient access to nutritious foods.\nIt's well-established that food prices are an important determinant of food choice. Low-income households report that they find it difficult to adopt dietary guidelines because food prices are a barrier to improving their diets.\nWhen researchers estimate the cost of diets people actually eat, higher-quality diets are typically more costly.\nWhile this may be so, it does not, in itself, prove that healthy diets are necessarily more expensive or cost-prohibitive. After all, not all socioeconomically disadvantaged people consume poor diets.\nWe can easily think of a number of foods and recipes that are both inexpensive and nutritious. The internet is full of recipes for \"eating well on a budget.\"", "52": "Research shows that in developed countries, more affluent and educated people tend to consume higher-quality diets—including more fruits and vegetables, fish and whole grains. On the contrary, economically disadvantaged people report diets that are nutrient-poor and energy-dense. They are less likely to have food-purchasing habits that conform to public health recommendations.\nThese dietary differences are often accompanied by higher rates of obesity and diabetes among lower-income people. This relationship between social class and diet quality and health is extensively documented. However, the research does not explain why this is the case—a question that has significant implications for designing effective policies and initiatives to improve diets and prevent chronic diseases.\nPublic-health initiatives to promote healthy diets often focus on providing nutrition education and recipes(食谱). These approaches, however, often presume less food literacy (i.e., food knowledge and skills) among low-income people. Are unhealthy diets really the result of poor choices, limited food skills and knowledge?\nResearch suggests that adults in food-insecure households are just as likely as those in food-secure households to adjust recipes to make them more healthy. They are also just as proficient in food preparation and cooking skills. There is no indication that increasing food skills or budgeting skills will reduce food insecurity.\nInstead, disadvantaged groups are constrained by their economic, material and social circumstances. For example, low income is the strongest predictor of food insecurity in Canada, where one in eight households experiences insufficient access to nutritious foods.\nIt's well-established that food prices are an important determinant of food choice. Low-income households report that they find it difficult to adopt dietary guidelines because food prices are a barrier to improving their diets.\nWhen researchers estimate the cost of diets people actually eat, higher-quality diets are typically more costly.\nWhile this may be so, it does not, in itself, prove that healthy diets are necessarily more expensive or cost-prohibitive. After all, not all socioeconomically disadvantaged people consume poor diets.\nWe can easily think of a number of foods and recipes that are both inexpensive and nutritious. The internet is full of recipes for \"eating well on a budget.\"", "53": "Research shows that in developed countries, more affluent and educated people tend to consume higher-quality diets—including more fruits and vegetables, fish and whole grains. On the contrary, economically disadvantaged people report diets that are nutrient-poor and energy-dense. They are less likely to have food-purchasing habits that conform to public health recommendations.\nThese dietary differences are often accompanied by higher rates of obesity and diabetes among lower-income people. This relationship between social class and diet quality and health is extensively documented. However, the research does not explain why this is the case—a question that has significant implications for designing effective policies and initiatives to improve diets and prevent chronic diseases.\nPublic-health initiatives to promote healthy diets often focus on providing nutrition education and recipes(食谱). These approaches, however, often presume less food literacy (i.e., food knowledge and skills) among low-income people. Are unhealthy diets really the result of poor choices, limited food skills and knowledge?\nResearch suggests that adults in food-insecure households are just as likely as those in food-secure households to adjust recipes to make them more healthy. They are also just as proficient in food preparation and cooking skills. There is no indication that increasing food skills or budgeting skills will reduce food insecurity.\nInstead, disadvantaged groups are constrained by their economic, material and social circumstances. For example, low income is the strongest predictor of food insecurity in Canada, where one in eight households experiences insufficient access to nutritious foods.\nIt's well-established that food prices are an important determinant of food choice. Low-income households report that they find it difficult to adopt dietary guidelines because food prices are a barrier to improving their diets.\nWhen researchers estimate the cost of diets people actually eat, higher-quality diets are typically more costly.\nWhile this may be so, it does not, in itself, prove that healthy diets are necessarily more expensive or cost-prohibitive. After all, not all socioeconomically disadvantaged people consume poor diets.\nWe can easily think of a number of foods and recipes that are both inexpensive and nutritious. The internet is full of recipes for \"eating well on a budget.\"", "54": "Research shows that in developed countries, more affluent and educated people tend to consume higher-quality diets—including more fruits and vegetables, fish and whole grains. On the contrary, economically disadvantaged people report diets that are nutrient-poor and energy-dense. They are less likely to have food-purchasing habits that conform to public health recommendations.\nThese dietary differences are often accompanied by higher rates of obesity and diabetes among lower-income people. This relationship between social class and diet quality and health is extensively documented. However, the research does not explain why this is the case—a question that has significant implications for designing effective policies and initiatives to improve diets and prevent chronic diseases.\nPublic-health initiatives to promote healthy diets often focus on providing nutrition education and recipes(食谱). These approaches, however, often presume less food literacy (i.e., food knowledge and skills) among low-income people. Are unhealthy diets really the result of poor choices, limited food skills and knowledge?\nResearch suggests that adults in food-insecure households are just as likely as those in food-secure households to adjust recipes to make them more healthy. They are also just as proficient in food preparation and cooking skills. There is no indication that increasing food skills or budgeting skills will reduce food insecurity.\nInstead, disadvantaged groups are constrained by their economic, material and social circumstances. For example, low income is the strongest predictor of food insecurity in Canada, where one in eight households experiences insufficient access to nutritious foods.\nIt's well-established that food prices are an important determinant of food choice. Low-income households report that they find it difficult to adopt dietary guidelines because food prices are a barrier to improving their diets.\nWhen researchers estimate the cost of diets people actually eat, higher-quality diets are typically more costly.\nWhile this may be so, it does not, in itself, prove that healthy diets are necessarily more expensive or cost-prohibitive. After all, not all socioeconomically disadvantaged people consume poor diets.\nWe can easily think of a number of foods and recipes that are both inexpensive and nutritious. The internet is full of recipes for \"eating well on a budget.\"", "55": "Research shows that in developed countries, more affluent and educated people tend to consume higher-quality diets—including more fruits and vegetables, fish and whole grains. On the contrary, economically disadvantaged people report diets that are nutrient-poor and energy-dense. They are less likely to have food-purchasing habits that conform to public health recommendations.\nThese dietary differences are often accompanied by higher rates of obesity and diabetes among lower-income people. This relationship between social class and diet quality and health is extensively documented. However, the research does not explain why this is the case—a question that has significant implications for designing effective policies and initiatives to improve diets and prevent chronic diseases.\nPublic-health initiatives to promote healthy diets often focus on providing nutrition education and recipes(食谱). These approaches, however, often presume less food literacy (i.e., food knowledge and skills) among low-income people. Are unhealthy diets really the result of poor choices, limited food skills and knowledge?\nResearch suggests that adults in food-insecure households are just as likely as those in food-secure households to adjust recipes to make them more healthy. They are also just as proficient in food preparation and cooking skills. There is no indication that increasing food skills or budgeting skills will reduce food insecurity.\nInstead, disadvantaged groups are constrained by their economic, material and social circumstances. For example, low income is the strongest predictor of food insecurity in Canada, where one in eight households experiences insufficient access to nutritious foods.\nIt's well-established that food prices are an important determinant of food choice. Low-income households report that they find it difficult to adopt dietary guidelines because food prices are a barrier to improving their diets.\nWhen researchers estimate the cost of diets people actually eat, higher-quality diets are typically more costly.\nWhile this may be so, it does not, in itself, prove that healthy diets are necessarily more expensive or cost-prohibitive. After all, not all socioeconomically disadvantaged people consume poor diets.\nWe can easily think of a number of foods and recipes that are both inexpensive and nutritious. The internet is full of recipes for \"eating well on a budget.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) constantly\n副词\n【词性槽】almost 后修饰频率，要副词\n【依据】almost constantly = 几乎一直，呼应高频上网\n上文 nearly half report using the internet 讲近一半青少年报告上网，almost constantly\n（几乎不间断地）正好量化这种高频，与第 2 段 Continuous __(27)__ 的\"持续\"语义一致。\n【竞争词】H) nationally 义不搭：全国性地无法修饰上网频率\nnationally 意为\"全国性地\"，almost nationally\"几乎全国性地\"语义不通，它只在 28 空\nnationally representative 那种搭配里成立。\nO) vaguely 义不搭：含糊地与上网频率无关\nvaguely\"含糊地、模糊地\"，almost vaguely 不构成\"几乎一直在用\"的频率义，与上文\"近一半人\n报告上网\"逻辑对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) usage\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 Continuous 后作主语中心词，要名词\n【依据】Continuous usage = 持续使用，承接上文\"花时间用机\"\nusage\"使用\"指持续使用手机。上文 many teens find much of that time to be\nunsatisfyingly spent，本句 Continuous __(27)__ shouldn't be mistaken for endless\nenjoyment 即\"持续的使用不该被误当作无尽的享受\"，usage 指代前文的用机时间，语义\n连贯。\n【竞争词】C) behavior 义偏宽：behavior 泛指行为，不如 usage 切题\nbehavior\"行为\"太泛，且要留给 34 空 model good __(34)__\"示范好行为\"那个更贴的语境；本\n句强调的是\"使用手机\"这一具体动作，usage 更准。\nE) context 义不搭：context 是\"环境\"，不被 continuous 修饰\ncontext\"环境、背景\"不能说\"持续的环境不该被误当作享受\"，语义不通，且它要留给 32 空\nliving in the __(32)__ 的语境。\nK) solution 义不搭：solution 是解决办法，与\"享受\"对不上\nsolution\"解决办法\"，\"持续的解决办法不该被误当作享受\"逻辑荒谬，排除。\nM) summary 义不搭：summary 是总结，与语境无关\nsummary\"总结、摘要\"，\"持续的总结\"与\"误当作享受\"毫无逻辑关系，排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) nationally\n副词\n【词性槽】形容词 representative 前要副词修饰\n【依据】nationally representative 是固定搭配，指全国代表性调查\n上文 A new __(28)__ representative survey about \"screen time and device\ndistractions\" from the Pew Research Center，皮尤研究中心的调查正是\"全国代表\n性\"的，nationally representative（具全国代表性的）是英语里描述抽样调查的常见固定\n说法。\n【竞争词】O) vaguely 搭配不成立：vaguely representative 不合调查语境\nvaguely\"含糊地\"修饰 representative 成\"含糊地有代表性\"，与权威机构皮尤的正式调查（讲究\n严谨抽样）感情色彩冲突，不是描述调查的常用搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) inseparable\n形容词\n【词性槽】副词 worryingly 后、接 from，作表语的形容词\n【依据】inseparable from = 与…分不开，正配 from their phones\ninseparable 搭配 be inseparable from something 意为\"与…密不可分\"。teenagers are\nworryingly __(29)__ from their phones 即\"青少年到了令人担忧地离不开手机的地步\"，介\n词 from 与 inseparable 精确对应，也呼应主旨\"沉迷手机\"。\n【竞争词】B) addicted 搭配错介词：addicted to 不接 from\naddicted\"上瘾的\"语义虽贴近\"沉迷\"，但固定搭配是 addicted to phones，不与 from 连用；\n本空后是 from，介词不符，必须排除——这是最具迷惑性的陷阱。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— addicted 义比 inseparable 更贴主旨，却配错介词。\nteenagers are worryingly __(29)__ from their phones 讲\"沉迷手机\"，addicted\"上瘾\"看\n着最切题，极易误选。防坑：空后是 from，addicted 只配 to，唯一配 from 的形容词是\ninseparable（inseparable from）。介词优先于词义。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) absorbed\n形容词\n【词性槽】spend time (being) ___ in，表状态的形容词\n【依据】be absorbed in 沉浸于，正配 in their phones\nabsorbed 的固定搭配 be absorbed in something 意为\"沉浸/专注于某事\"，spend too\nmuch time __(30)__ in their phones 即\"花太多时间沉浸在手机里\"，介词 in 与 absorbed\n精确对应。\n【竞争词】B) addicted 搭配错介词：addicted to 不是 to in\naddicted\"上瘾的\"虽语义沾边，但固定搭配是 addicted to phones，不与 in 连用；本空后是\nin，介词不符，排除。\nG) inseparable 搭配错介词：inseparable from 不接 in\ninseparable\"密不可分的\"搭配 from，且它要留给 29 空 worryingly __(29)__ from their\nphones 那个接 from 的语境。\nL) specific 义不搭：specific 与\"沉浸手机\"无关\nspecific\"特定的\"既不与 in 搭表状态，语义上\"花时间 specific 在手机里\"也讲不通，明显排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) specific\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 is 后作表语，要形容词\n【依据】be specific to = 为…所特有，配 hardly 表\"并非青少年独有\"\nspecific 搭配 be specific to something 意为\"为…所特有\"。上文 Rideout 说这一现象 She\nsays it's hardly __(31)__ to teenagers\"几乎并非青少年所特有\"，下句紧接 They are\ndealing with the same challenges that adults are\"他们面对的和成年人一样\"，正说明问\n题不是青少年独有，specific 精确对应。\n【竞争词】B) addicted 逻辑指向反：hardly addicted 与下文矛盾\naddicted\"上瘾的\"配 to 虽语法通，但\"几乎不对青少年上瘾\"与下文 living in the __(32)__ of a\ntech environment designed to suck as much of their time\"环境设计就是吸走时间\"的沉迷论\n调相反，逻辑冲突。\nG) inseparable 搭配错介词：inseparable from 不接 to\ninseparable 搭配 from 而非 to，且\"几乎与青少年不可分\"语义也别扭，留给 29 空接 from 的\n语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) context\n名词\n【词性槽】in the ___ of 结构，定冠词后要名词\n【依据】in the context of = 在…的环境/背景下\ncontext\"环境、背景\"搭配 in the context of 意为\"在…背景/环境下\"。living in the\n__(32)__ of a tech environment designed to suck as much of their time\"生活在一个被\n设计来吸走时间的科技环境之中\"，context 与后面的 tech environment 同指，搭配地道。\n【竞争词】C) behavior 义不搭：in the behavior of 不合\"科技环境\"语境\nbehavior\"行为\"放进 in the ___ of a tech environment 成\"在科技环境的行为里\"，语义别扭，\n且 behavior 要留给 34 空。\nK) solution 义不搭：in the solution of 与环境对不上\nsolution\"解决办法\"，\"生活在科技环境的解决办法里\"不通，排除。\nM) summary 义不搭：in the summary of 语义荒谬\nsummary\"总结\"，\"生活在科技环境的总结里\"完全讲不通，排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) shape\n动词\n【词性槽】助动词 can 后、主语 way 之后缺谓语动词\n【依据】shape the way = 塑造（互动）方式\nshape 作动词意为\"塑造、影响\"，shape the way they interact 即\"塑造他们与孩子互动的\n方式\"，与上文 The way parents interact with technology 形成\"家长用科技的方式 → 影\n响 → 与孩子互动的方式\"的因果，语义贴切。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) behavior\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 good 后作 model 的宾语中心词，要名词\n【依据】model good behavior = 示范良好行为\nbehavior\"行为、举止\"。it's up to parents to model good __(34)__ 即\"该由家长示范良好\n的行为\"，下文 Kids tend to take note if their parents put their phone away at\ndinner\"孩子会注意家长饭桌上放下手机\"正是 good behavior 的例证，语义吻合。\n【竞争词】K) solution 义不搭：model good solution 不合\"示范\"语境\nsolution\"解决办法\"，\"示范良好的解决办法\"搭配生硬，且下文举的是\"放下手机\"这种行为，不\n是某种方案，排除。\nM) summary 义不搭：good summary 与\"示范给孩子\"无关\nsummary\"总结\"，\"示范良好的总结\"语义不通，排除。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— solution 沾\"手机问题\"主旨，但下文例证指向行为。to\nmodel good __(34)__，全文围绕\"手机问题\"，solution\"解决办法\"易被误选。防坑：下文\nKids tend to take note if their parents put their phone away at dinner 举的是具体行\n为，model good behavior 才对得上例证。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) absorbed", "B) addicted", "C) behavior", "D) constantly", "E) context", "F) exercise", "G) inseparable", "H) nationally", "I) recruited", "J) shape", "K) solution", "L) specific", "M) summary", "N) usage", "O) vaguely"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) exercise\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can 后要动词原形\n【依据】exercise control 行使控制，固定搭配\nexercise 作动词有\"行使、运用\"义，exercise control over... 是固定搭配，意为\"对…行使掌\n控\"。上文 realize that they can __(35)__ some more control over their devices 正是说\n孩子能对自己的设备多一些掌控，搭配完全吻合。\n【竞争词】J) shape 搭配不当：shape control 不成立\nshape 作动词意为\"塑造、影响\"，常接 the way、behavior 等抽象事物，但\"shape control\nover devices\"不是英语习惯搭配，且 shape 要留给 33 空 can __(33)__ the way they interact\nwith their kids 这个\"塑造方式\"的典型语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "A behavior that does not conform to social norms may be described as being deviant.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不合社会规范即\"越轨\"的定义\n【定位】However, deviance can also describe a behavior that simply differs from the norm.\n然而，越轨也可以指一种仅仅与常规不同的行为。\n【改写】does not conform to social norms ↔ differs from the norm\n\"不符合规范\"＝\"与常规不同\"\ndescribed as being deviant ↔ deviance\n形容词与名词同根", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Various experiments found that participants who cheated in the initial task performed better in the creativity test.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】首个任务作弊者创造力测试更好\n【定位】For every one of the five studies, they found the same thing—participants who cheated in the\nfirst task did better on the creativity task.\n在这五项研究的每一项中，他们都发现了同一件事——在第一个任务中作弊的被试在创造力\n任务上表现得更好。\n【改写】Various experiments ↔ every one of the five studies\n\"多个实验\"概括\"五项研究\"\ncheated in the initial task ↔ cheated in the first task\ninitial＝first\nperformed better in the creativity test ↔ did better on the creativity task\n\"表现更好\"同义\n【辨邻】 G wanted to examine whether people who behave unethically in one task are\nmore creative 是研究意图，是问句式的待检验假设，并没有陈述\"作弊者确实表现更\n好\"这一结果，故不是本句源头。\nwanted to examine whether people who behave unethically in one task are more creative than\nothers on a subsequent task", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "People may be simply considered evil if their behaviors are morally unacceptable to us.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】行为道德上不可接受即被当作恶\n【定位】If their actions deviate substantially from what we consider acceptable, we may label\nthem evil.\n如果他人的行为大幅偏离我们认为可接受的范围，我们就可能给他们贴上\"恶\"的标签。\n【改写】their behaviors are morally unacceptable to us ↔\ntheir actions deviate substantially from what we consider acceptable\n\"道德上不可接受\"＝\"偏离我们认为可接受的\"\nconsidered evil ↔ label them evil\n\"被当作恶\"＝\"贴上恶标签\"\n【辨邻】 D Evil behavior is typically thought to embrace one or both forms 是在归类恶行，\n并未陈述\"行为不可接受我们就当其为恶\"这一主观判定过程，故不是源头段。\nEvil is typically referred to when there is deviance from social norms", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The research published by two scientists was intended to examine the relationship between dishonesty and creativity.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】两位科学家发表的研究查\"不诚实与创造力\"关系\n【定位】In a research paper, Evil Genius, published in 2014, the behavioral scientists Francesca Gino\nand Scott Wiltermuth wanted to examine whether people who behave unethically in one task\nare more creative than others on a subsequent task, even after controlling for differences in\nbaseline creative skills. The unethical behavior they chose was dishonesty.\n在 2014 年发表的研究论文《邪恶天才》中，行为科学家 Francesca Gino 与 Scott\nWiltermuth 想检验：在一个任务中行为不端的人，是否在后续任务中比他人更有创造力\n（即便已控制了基线创造力差异）。他们所选的不端行为是不诚实。\n【改写】two scientists ↔ Francesca Gino and Scott Wiltermuth\n具名两人＝\"两位科学家\"\nexamine the relationship between dishonesty and creativity ↔\nwanted to examine whether people who behave unethically in one task are more creative\n\"检验……关系\"＝\"检验是否更有创造力\"\n【辨邻】 J they found the same thing—participants who cheated in the first task did\nbetter 是已得出的结果，而题干强调研究\"意图检验\"（was intended to examine），\n落点应在提出研究目的的 G 段。\nFor every one of the five studies, they found the same thing", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The author's lectures sparked lively discussions in his class.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作者的讲课引发热烈课堂讨论\n【定位】It was a huge success. The debates were heated and interesting.\n那门课非常成功。课上的辩论既热烈又有趣。\n【改写】lively discussions in his class ↔ The debates were heated and interesting\n\"热烈讨论\"＝\"激烈而有趣的辩论\"\nThe author's lectures ↔ teaching a university class on evil\n\"作者的讲课\"＝\"作者教的大学课\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "The researchers tested the participants' creativity by asking them to play a word game.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】用文字游戏测被试创造力\n【定位】After each task, the researchers measured participants' performance on the Remote Associates\nTest. This shows participants three words at a time that appear to be unrelated, and the person\nhas to think of a fourth word that is associated with all of them.\n每个任务之后，研究者用\"远距离联想测验\"测量被试的表现。该测验每次给被试三个看似无\n关的词，要求其想出一个与三者都相关的第四个词。\n【改写】play a word game ↔ three words at a time\n\"文字游戏\"概括三词联想\ntested the participants' creativity ↔ you need to be creative\n\"测创造力\"＝\"做对需要有创造力\"\n【辨邻】 H they were given matrices and had to find two numbers that added up to 10 这\n项任务的作用是看被试是否谎报成绩、即测作弊，并非测创造力，故不是本句源头。\nParticipants were asked to self-report how well they did", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "It is time we realized that deviance may be capable of doing both good and harm to individuals and society.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】是时候认识越轨能同时为善与为害\n【定位】It's time for us to understand deviance, and realize its potential for good as well as for harm.\n是时候让我们理解越轨，并认识到它兼具向善与为害的潜力了。\n【改写】It is time we realized ↔ It's time for us to understand\n\"是时候认识到\"同义\ndoing both good and harm ↔ its potential for good as well as for harm\n\"既善又害\"＝\"向善与为害的潜力\"\n【辨邻】 F Great benefit and great harm can come from the same human tendency 说的是\n创造力一面的利害，并非题干\"是时候认识到越轨能同时为善为害\"这一对越轨整体的总\n括，故不是源头段。\nGreat benefit and great harm can come from the same human tendency", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The reasons for people's evil behaviors can be explained in more ways than one.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】人作恶的原因不止一种\n【定位】But if we take a good look, the word \"evil\" is insufficient—there are no simple explanations\nfor why humans do bad things: instead there are many, and they are all marvelously different.\n但只要细看，\"恶\"这个词是不够的——人为什么做坏事并无简单的解释：相反，解释有许多\n种，而且各不相同、奇妙各异。\n【改写】people's evil behaviors ↔ why humans do bad things\n\"作恶\"＝\"做坏事\"\nexplained in more ways than one ↔\nthere are no simple explanations for why humans do bad things\\: instead there are many\n\"不止一种解释\"＝\"解释有许多\"\n【辨邻】 B Calling someone evil is often similar to saying they cannot change 讲的是贴标签\n的危险，并没有陈述\"作恶的原因有多种\"，故不是本句源头。\nWith others, however, it is far easier to write them off", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The math task in one experiment was designed to test participants' tendency to cheat.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】某实验里的数学任务用来测作弊倾向\n【定位】Over five experiments, researchers gave participants tasks in which they could cheat. In one\nstudy, they were given matrices and had to find two numbers that added up to 10. Participants\nwere asked to self-report how well they did at the end of the study: 59% cheated by saying\nthat they solved more matrices than they actually had.\n在五个实验中，研究者给被试设计了可以作弊的任务。在其中一项研究里，被试拿到矩阵，\n要找出相加等于 10 的两个数。被试被要求在研究结束时自报成绩：59% 的人谎报自己解出\n的矩阵数量、即作弊。\n【改写】The math task ↔ matrices and had to find two numbers that added up to 10\n\"数学任务\"＝矩阵找数相加\ndesigned to test participants' tendency to cheat ↔ tasks in which they could cheat\n\"测作弊倾向\"＝\"设计成可作弊的任务\"\n【辨邻】 I you need to be creative 表明 I 段联想测验衡量的是创造力，而非作弊倾向，故不是\n本句源头。\nthe researchers measured participants' performance on the Remote Associates Test", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some creative ideas have turned out to do harm to human society.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】某些创造性想法最终危害人类社会\n【定位】Thinking creatively has given us modern medicine, technology and modern political\nstructures, but it has also given us poison and nuclear weapons.\n创造性思维给了我们现代医学、技术与现代政治制度，但也给了我们毒药和核武器。\n【改写】Some creative ideas ↔ Thinking creatively\n\"创造性想法\"＝\"创造性思维\"\ndo harm to human society ↔ it has also given us poison and nuclear weapons\n抽象\"危害社会\"由\"毒药核武\"实例承载\n【辨邻】 J being creative involves \"thinking outside the box\" 讲的是创造的思维特点与作弊相\n通，并没有陈述\"创造的成果危害人类社会\"，故不是源头段。\nbeing creative involves \"thinking outside the box\", deviating from expectations", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What has Bill Thomas been doing for a quarter of a century?", "options": ["A) Transforming people's lifestyle.", "B) Honoring his Harvard education.", "C) Changing people's philosophy of life.", "D) Shifting people's perspective on aging."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What has Bill Thomas been doing for a quarter of a century? 用 what 直接问做了什\n么事，且锁定了二十五年的具体时间，没有 infer/suggest/imply 等推断信号，也不问全文\n主旨。\n【拆句】现在完成进行时 has been doing 强调「一直在做、且仍在继续」，for a quarter of a\ncentury 是时间段状语；对应原文要找「持续二十五年都在做的那件事」。\n【定位】Even though we are living in an age where growing old is thought of as an inevitable misery,\nthis doctor has been changing the game for seniors over the last 25 years.\n尽管我们生活在一个把变老视为难免之苦的时代，这位医生在过去 25 年里一直在为老年人\n改变这一局面。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nTransforming people\\'s lifestyle 偷换了 changing the game 的对象——原文改变的\n是观念/局面，不是 lifestyle（衣食住行的生活方式）。\nB) 无中生有\nHonoring his Harvard education 原文虽提 the Harvard-educated physician，但只\n是介绍背景，并未说他二十五年所做的事是「为哈佛教育争光」。\nC) 以偏概全\nChanging people\\'s philosophy of life 把「对变老的看法」扩大为「整套人生哲\n学」，范围被无限放大，且对象从 seniors 变成了 people 全体。\nD) ✓ 正确\ngrowing old is thought of as an inevitable misery ↔ perspectives on aging\nchanging the game for seniors ↔ Shifting people\\'s perspective\n预判说他在改变「对变老的看法」，Shifting people\\'s perspective on aging 在字面\n与语义上同时复现这一判断，无须额外推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why did Bill Thomas try something different in the nursing home?", "options": ["A) He wanted to make it more pleasant for seniors.", "B) He wanted to apply his Harvard training to practice.", "C) He felt it his duty to revolutionize its management.", "D) He felt disappointed working in the environment."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why did Bill Thomas try something different in the nursing home? 用 why 直接问动\n机，原文有明确的因果或心理描写句即可定位，不必跨段推断。\n【拆句】一般过去时 did，问的是过去某次具体动机；try something different 是「尝试不一样的做\n法」，对应原文「引入动植物」这一动作。\n【定位】The depressing and regimented(严格管制的) environment got him thinking on what exactly\ncould improve the residents' conditions.\n那种压抑而管制严格的环境促使他思考究竟怎样才能改善住户的处境。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nimprove the residents\\' conditions ↔ make it more pleasant for seniors\n预判说「让老人住得更舒心」，make it more pleasant for seniors 在动宾结构与对象\n层面同时复现，无需推断。\nB) 无中生有\napply his Harvard training to practice 把 Harvard-educated physician 这一背景细\n节拼到动机上——原文从未说他这么做是为了「把哈佛所学落地」。\nC) 过度推断\nfelt it his duty to revolutionize its management 把「想改善老人处境」夸张成「觉得\n自己有责任去革命化养老院管理」，原文既无「职责感」亦无「revolutionize\nmanagement」的表述。\nD) 张冠李戴\nfelt disappointed working in the environment 把 The depressing and\nregimented(严格管制的) environment 这一对环境的描写，错置成他个人情绪。原文\n是环境压抑，而非他心情失望；且这是触发思考的背景，不等于动机本身。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do we learn about Bill Thomas bringing animals and plants into the nursing home?", "options": ["A) He made a mess of the nursing home.", "B) He did something all professionals would do.", "C) He won instant support from the state authorities.", "D) He acted in violation of the state law."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about Bill Thomas bringing animals and plants into the nursing\nhome? 锁定一个具体事件，问围绕它能从原文得到什么信息，无推断标记。\n【拆句】动名词短语 bringing… into… 作介词 about 的宾语，整句问「关于他往养老院里搬动植物\n这件事，我们能知道什么」。\n【定位】Even though animals in nursing homes were illegal at the time, Dr. Bill Thomas took\na chance.\n尽管当时养老院里养动物是违法的，比尔·托马斯医生还是冒了险（这么做了）。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nmade a mess of the nursing home 把引入动植物说成「把养老院弄得乱七八糟」，与\n原文完全相反——原文紧接着是 dramatic decrease in death rates 和 the residents\nwere simply happier，效果是正面的。\nB) 与原文相反\ndid something all professionals would do 说这是「所有专业人士都会做的事」，但\n原文 Dr. Bill Thomas took a chance 与 animals in nursing homes were illegal at\nthe time 都指向「冒险特例」，并非行业常规。\nC) 与原文相反\nwon instant support from the state authorities 把 took a chance（冒险）反向虚构\n成「立即获得州政府支持」，与原文「违法」事实完全冲突。\nD) ✓ 正确\nanimals in nursing homes were illegal at the time ↔ acted in violation of the state law\n预判抓住「违法」这一事实，acted in violation of the state law 用名词 violation＋介\n宾 of the state law 复现了 illegal，语义重心一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What has Bill Thomas been persistently advocating?", "options": ["A) Good health is not just a privilege of the young.", "B) Nursing homes should be strictly limited in size.", "C) Getting old is by no means something miserable.", "D) Residences for seniors should be run independently."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What has Bill Thomas been persistently advocating? 问的是他一贯主张的具体内容；原\n文有「preaching a singular message」句可直接对接，仍属细节题。\n【拆句】现在完成进行时 has been advocating＋副词 persistently 强调持续性；what 是\nadvocating 的宾语前置。\n【定位】Thomas, now a speaker and author of several books, also created small, independently-run\nresidences with their own bedrooms and bathrooms, and he has been preaching a singular\nmessage that getting old is not a bad thing.\n如今他既是演讲者又是几本书的作者，他建立了独立运营、带独立卧室和卫生间的小型住\n所，并一直在传播一个核心讯息：变老并不是一件坏事。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nGood health is not just a privilege of the young 把话题从「对变老的看法」偷换成\n「健康权」；原文虽提 50\\% drop in medical prescriptions，但讨论的是养老院环境\n改善的效果，并未上升为「健康是不是年轻人专属」的命题。\nB) 以偏概全\nNursing homes should be strictly limited in size 借用 the buildings are so big 这\n一例证，伪装成他的「长期主张」。原文只是用大养老院解释轮椅多，并未上升为「养\n老院必须严格限制规模」的口号。\nC) ✓ 正确\ngetting old is not a bad thing ↔ Getting old is by no means something miserable\nthink of their age as an enriching new phase of life rather than the end of it ↔\nGetting old is by no means something miserable\n预判核心是「变老不糟糕」，Getting old is by no means something miserable 在主\n谓宾与否定逻辑上完全复现这一判断。\nD) 以偏概全\nResidences for seniors should be run independently 借用 created small,\nindependently-run residences 这一具体做法，伪装成他的中心主张；但 preach a\nsingular message 后的同位语清楚说明他反复在传的核心讯息是 getting old is not a\nbad thing，而非「独立运营」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "How is Bill Thomas's new concept received?", "options": ["A) It is gaining ground in many countries.", "B) It is being heatedly debated worldwide.", "C) It is considered revolutionary everywhere.", "D) It is winning approval from the government."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How is Bill Thomas\\'s new concept received? 问「被如何接受」，原文必有一句直陈反\n响的句子，无须跨段推断。\n【拆句】一般现在时被动语态 is received，主语是 new concept，问外界对其评价；how 在句首作\n方式状语。\n【定位】The 56-year-old doctor's methods have been adopted in Australia, Japan, Canada, and\nAmerica with enormous success.\n这位 56 岁的医生的方法已经在澳大利亚、日本、加拿大和美国得到采纳，并大获成功。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nhave been adopted in Australia, Japan, Canada, and America ↔\ngaining ground in many countries\nwith enormous success ↔ gaining ground\n预判强调「被多国接受、正在扩展」，gaining ground in many countries 同时复现\n「多国」与「势头良好」两个要点。\nB) 与原文相反\nbeing heatedly debated worldwide 把「被接受」反扭成「被激烈争论」，原文\nenormous success 是正向评价，没有任何争议、反对的语义。\nC) 以偏概全\nconsidered revolutionary everywhere 既无原文支持的「革命性」评价，又把多国采\n用扩大为「everywhere」——原文只列了四国，不能等同于「到处」。\nD) 无中生有\nwinning approval from the government 凭空虚构「获得政府批准」，原文只说被采\n用并成功，未指明任何一个政府机构层面的官方批准。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What can we learn from research on diets in developed countries?", "options": ["A) Dietary recommendations are not fit for underprivileged people.", "B) People from different social groups vary in their dietary habits.", "C) People's choice of food depends on their individual taste", "D) There is no consensus on what high-quality diets are."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What can we learn from research on diets in developed countries 没有 infer/imply 等\n推断信号，问的是研究的客观结论。\n【拆句】题干 What can we learn from research on diets in developed countries 是 What 引导\n的特殊疑问句，主干是 What can we learn，后接介词短语 from research on diets in\ndeveloped countries 修饰 learn 的来源；无从句、无否定、无陷阱。\n【定位】Research shows that in developed countries, more affluent and educated people tend to\nconsume higher-quality diets—including more fruits and vegetables, fish and whole grains.\nOn the contrary, economically disadvantaged people report diets that are nutrient-poor and\nenergy-dense.\n研究表明，在发达国家，更富裕且受过良好教育的人倾向于消费更高质量的饮食——包括\n更多水果蔬菜、鱼类和全谷物；相反，经济弱势人群所报告的饮食则是营养贫乏而能量密\n集的。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文只说弱势群体 less likely to have food-purchasing habits that conform to\npublic health recommendations，是\"他们不照做\"，并非 Dietary\nrecommendations are not fit for underprivileged people 这种\"建议本身不适合\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmore affluent and educated people tend to consume higher-quality diets ... On the contrary,\neconomically disadvantaged people report diets that are nutrient-poor and energy-dense\n↔ People from different social groups vary in their dietary habits\n预判中的\"不同社会群体在饮食上存在差异\"恰好被 People from different social\ngroups vary in their dietary habits 直接覆盖，匹配度最高。\nC) 无中生有\n全段讨论的是阶级、收入、教育对饮食的影响，没有任何关于 individual taste 的\n论述。\nD) 无中生有\n原文以列举 fruits and vegetables, fish and whole grains 等方式默认了高质量饮食的\n内涵，没有讨论是否存在 consensus。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author say is important in formulating policies to improve diets and health?", "options": ["A) A better understanding of the relationship between social class and health.", "B) A greater emphasis on studying the cause of obesity and chronic diseases.", "C) Prioritizing the provision of better nutrition for lower classes.", "D) Designing education programs and initiatives on public health."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say is important 直接索取作者的明示判断，不是要考生自己\n推断。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say is important in formulating policies to improve diets\nand health 是嵌套疑问句，主语 the author，谓语 say，宾语为省略 that 的从句 What ...\nis important in formulating policies，to improve diets and health 是不定式作目的状\n语，结构清楚不绕。\n【定位】However, the research does not explain why this is the case—a question that has significant\nimplications for designing effective policies and initiatives to improve diets and prevent\nchronic diseases.\n然而，研究并未解释\"为何如此\"——而这恰是一个对设计有效政策、推动饮食改善与慢病预\n防具有重大意义的问题。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe research does not explain why this is the case—a question that has significant implications\nfor designing effective policies\n↔ A better understanding of the relationship between social class and health\n预判的\"弄清阶级与健康关系的成因\"被 A better understanding of the relationship\nbetween social class and health 一句直接对应。\nB) 以偏概全\n原文谈的是 designing effective policies 的关键，是\"理解关系成因\"；选项却把焦点收\n缩到 cause of obesity and chronic diseases，丢掉了\"社会阶级\"这一关键。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文强调政策制定需要先理解\"为何如此\"，并没有主张直接 Prioritizing the provision\nof better nutrition。\nD) 张冠李戴\neducation programs and initiatives on public health 是第三段才出现的话题\nnutrition education，且作者后文持质疑态度，并非他认定为政策制定的关键。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does research reveal about adults in food-insecure households?", "options": ["A) Their eating habits need to be changed.", "B) Their food literacy has been improving.", "C) They do not pay much attention to their food recipes.", "D) They do not lack food knowledge or budgeting skills."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does research reveal about adults in food-insecure households 问的是研究客观\n结论，正确项是原文一句的同义改写。\n【拆句】题干 What does research reveal about adults in food-insecure households 主干\nresearch reveal，宾语为 What，about adults in food-insecure households 是宾语补足\n的范围状语，限定研究对象，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Research suggests that adults in food-insecure households are just as likely as those in food-\nsecure households to adjust recipes to make them more healthy. They are also just as\nproficient in food preparation and cooking skills. There is no indication that increasing food\nskills or budgeting skills will reduce food insecurity.\n研究表明，食物不安全家庭的成年人，在调整食谱以使其更健康方面，与食物安全家庭的成\n年人一样可能；他们在食物准备与烹饪技能上也同样熟练；没有迹象表明提升食物技能或预\n算技能能减少食物不安全。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文整段都在论证\"他们的饮食与烹饪并无问题\"，Their eating habits need to be\nchanged 反而把矛头指向他们的饮食习惯。\nB) 无中生有\n原文说他们\"已经一样熟练\"，并未谈到 has been improving 这种\"正在提升\"的进程。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文明确说他们 adjust recipes to make them more healthy，反而是在认真对待食\n谱；do not pay much attention to their food recipes 与之相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\njust as proficient in food preparation and cooking skills ↔\nThey do not lack food knowledge or budgeting skills\nThere is no indication that increasing food skills or budgeting skills will reduce food insecurity\n↔ They do not lack food knowledge or budgeting skills\n预判中的\"不缺食物知识与预算技能\"与 They do not lack food knowledge or\nbudgeting skills 是肯定—否定的等值改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What would help improve food security among the disadvantaged groups in Canada?", "options": ["A) Teaching them budgeting skills.", "B) Increasing their food choices.", "C) Enabling them to have more access to nutritious foods.", "D) Taking more effective measures to increase food supplies."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What would help improve food security among the disadvantaged groups in\nCanada 问对应措施，原文应有明确陈述，不必推断。\n【拆句】题干 What would help improve food security among the disadvantaged groups in\nCanada 主干 What would help improve food security，help 后省略 to，among the\ndisadvantaged groups in Canada 是限定 food security 范围的介词短语，三层修饰但平\n铺直叙。\n【定位】For example, low income is the strongest predictor of food insecurity in Canada, where one in\neight households experiences insufficient access to nutritious foods.\n举例来说，在加拿大，低收入是食物不安全最强的预测因子，那里有八分之一的家庭无法充\n分获取营养食物。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n第四段已明确 no indication that increasing food skills or budgeting skills will\nreduce food insecurity，作者直接驳斥 Teaching them budgeting skills 这种做法。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是 insufficient access to nutritious foods 即\"获取渠道不足\"，不是\nIncreasing their food choices 即\"扩大可选范围\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\none in eight households experiences insufficient access to nutritious foods ↔\nEnabling them to have more access to nutritious foods\n预判中的\"让他们更多获取营养食物\"与 Enabling them to have more access to\nnutritious foods 一一对应。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文聚焦弱势人群的低收入与获取不足，并未主张 increase food supplies 这种宏观供\n给端的对策。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest disadvantaged people do to improve their health?", "options": ["A) Adopt a positive attitude towards dietary guidelines.", "B) Choose diets that are both healthy and affordable.", "C) Make sure to purchase healthy foods on the internet.", "D) Change their eating habits and consumption patterns."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest disadvantaged people do to improve their health 问\n作者明示的建议，不是要考生跨段推断。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author suggest disadvantaged people do to improve their\nhealth 是含 suggest 的特殊疑问句，宾语从句省略了 that 与 should，故 do 以原形出\n现；to improve their health 是不定式目的状语，整体结构清晰。\n【定位】We can easily think of a number of foods and recipes that are both inexpensive and nutritious.\nThe internet is full of recipes for \"eating well on a budget.\"\n我们可以轻易想到一些既便宜又有营养的食物与食谱；互联网上充满了\"在预算内吃得好\"的\n食谱。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只提到弱势群体 find it difficult to adopt dietary guidelines，并未让他们 Adopt\na positive attitude towards dietary guidelines。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfoods and recipes that are both inexpensive and nutritious ↔\nChoose diets that are both healthy and affordable\neating well on a budget ↔ Choose diets that are both healthy and affordable\n预判的\"选择既健康又负担得起的饮食\"与 Choose diets that are both healthy and\naffordable 是 both…and… 结构的整体同义改写。\nC) 过度推断\n原文只是说 The internet is full of recipes for \"eating well on a budget.\"，是举例说\n明网上有这类食谱，并非建议人们\"必须\" Make sure to purchase healthy foods on\nthe internet。\nD) 与原文相反\n作者反复说明弱势群体的饮食问题不出在饮食习惯本身，而在经济与获取层面，Change\ntheir eating habits and consumption patterns 把矛头重新指向习惯，与全文立场\n相悖。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2022-12-2"});
